# A magusfile's top level is arbitrary Buzz and runs unconditionally at workspace
# load - before `magus ls` (or any other command) even picks a target. A failure
# there used to reach the reader as whatever the underlying cause happened to say;
# this pins that every load failure is now a coded, pointable MGS1023 diagnostic
# naming the failing project, on both `magus ls` and `magus doctor`.

! exec magus ls
stderr 'MGS1023'
stderr 'boom'
! stdout .

# doctor's "workspace" check runs the SAME load ls does (not a parse-only pass),
# so it fails with the coded diagnostic too instead of reporting the workspace
# healthy on a magusfile that never actually loaded.
! exec magus doctor
stdout 'workspace: \[MGS1023\]'
stdout 'boom'
! stdout 'magusfile syntax'

-- magusfile.buzz --
throw "boom";
